            <ArticleTitle>Treatment of low back pain: myths and facts.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>499-503</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="QUESTION" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">Which are the most effective treatments for acute and chronic low back pain?</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="METHODS" NlmCategory="METHODS">Systematic reviews were conducted including identification and selection of relevant trials, assessment of the methodological quality, data extraction (study populations, interventions, and outcomes), and data analysis.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">acute low back pain. Nonsteroidal anti-inflammatory drugs (NSAIDs) and muscle relaxants relieve pain more than placebo; advice to stay active speeds up recovery and reduces chronic disability; bed rest and specific back exercises are not effective.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">chronic low back pain. Exercise therapy, back schools, behavioral therapy, and multidisciplinary programs are effective.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSIONS" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">Scientific evidence shows that an active approach to low back pain patients is effective.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
